\section*{Automorphism Problem for the Turing Degrees}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 241; 2026-09-24), the asserted result is that an order-preserving bijection of Turing degrees acts trivially on every degree.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 241; 2026-09-24), an arithmetic representation theorem turns an arbitrary degree automorphism into a Borel function on sets. Category cone avoidance removes auxiliary parameters from bounds on comeager pairs, and recovery from four values identifies the original degree.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The result supplies the conclusion in this problem with its displayed hypotheses and quantifiers. The broader statements and applications in the manuscript are kept separate from the particular question above. The supporting result is manuscript 1 (family 241; 2026-09-24).

\subsection{Formal verification}
For manuscript 1 (family 241; 2026-09-24), OpenAI lists Lean statements for this assertion: an order-preserving bijection of Turing degrees acts trivially on every degree.

The selected statement includes the clause used for this problem.

The linked family Comparator checks are \texttt{DegreeRigidity.lean}.

Their named declarations include \texttt{OAI.TuringRigidity.ManuscriptMain.rigidity}.

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

\subsection{Status caveat}
The manuscripts were produced by an internal OpenAI model. OpenAI cautions that results without formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently checked the proofs; the label records the scope of OpenAI's claim.

